            0.086,
            0.884,
            0.151
          ],
          "content": "**Q.6. Now mark any four points on your paper so that no three of them are on one line. Label them A, B, C, D. Draw all possible lines going through pairs of these points. How many lines do you get? Name them. How many angles can you name using A, B, C, D? Write them all down, and mark each of them with a curve as in Fig. 2.9.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.173,
            0.556,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": "↔↔"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.218,
            0.577,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_45_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.429,
            0.88,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": "Using A, B, C & D we can name the following angles $\\angle BAC ,   \\angle CAD ,   \\angle BAD ,   \\angle ADB ,$ ∠BDC, ∠ADC, ∠DCA, ∠ACB, ∠DCB, ∠CBD, ∠DBA & ∠CBA"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.499,
            0.219,
            0.516
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Section 2.6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.529,
            0.582,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "**Page 20 Is it always easy to compare two angles?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.559,
            0.882,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "No. it is not always easy to compare two angles. For eg. $8 9 ^ { \\circ }$ & 91º angles cannot be compared without measuring or overlapping. But for the given figures, comparison is easy."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.603,
            0.192,
            0.621
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Page 23"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.63,
            0.605,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": "嶽 Where else do we use superimposition to compare?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.648,
            0.76,
            0.666
          ],
          "content": "A few examples are- line segments, squares and circles. Think of more."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.666,
            0.299,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": "嶽 **Figure it out**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.72,
            0.882,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "**Q.1. Fold a rectangular sheet of paper, then draw a line along the fold created. Name and compare the angles formed between the fold and the sides of the paper. Make different angles by folding a rectangular sheet of paper and compare the angles. Which is the largest and smallest angle you made?**"
        },
